# Two env files, on purpose. Both are gitignored via `.env*.local`.
#
#   .env.local              -> the HOSTED project. Used by `next build` and
#                              production. Copy this file to create it:
#                              Supabase dashboard > project settings > API.
#
#   .env.development.local  -> the LOCAL stack. Used by `npm run dev`, because
#                              Next resolves env files in this order and stops
#                              at the first hit:
#                                process.env
#                                .env.development.local
#                                .env.local
#                                .env.development
#                                .env
#
# So dev talks to the local stack by default and nobody has to remember a flag.
# Create it from the values `supabase status` prints:
#
#   npx supabase start
#   npx supabase status -o env      # API_URL and PUBLISHABLE_KEY
#
# lib/env-guard.ts enforces this: `next dev` throws at boot if the URL is not
# local, because the failure mode otherwise is silent — the app renders the
# hosted project's data and nothing says so. Set
# ALLOW_REMOTE_SUPABASE_IN_DEV=1 to develop against the hosted project on
# purpose.
#
# The publishable/anon key is the only key the Next app should ever hold. The
# service-role key belongs in Edge Function secrets only.

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=your-project-url
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=your-publishable-or-anon-key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .env.deployed.local     -> the projects `npm run test:deployed` checks.
#
# A third file, because that suite's target is plural and is NOT the project
# the app is currently pointed at. It used to read .env.local, which names dev
# — so "compliance checks against the deployed project" never once looked at
# production, and production sat with public sign-up enabled and 12 unpushed
# migrations until an outage found it on 2026-09-02.
#
# Discovered by convention: every DEPLOYED_PROJECT_<NAME>_URL is paired with
# DEPLOYED_PROJECT_<NAME>_KEY. A project named "prod" is required — the suite
# refuses to run without it, so coverage cannot be quietly dropped.
#
# Publishable/anon keys only. Those ship in the browser bundle already, and
# nothing in tests/deployed can read a row: PostgREST resolves column and table
# names before it checks grants, so the suite detects a missing column (400) or
# table (404) while RLS still refuses it every row (401). Never put the
# service-role key here.
#
#   npx supabase projects api-keys --project-ref <ref>
#
# DEPLOYED_PROJECT_DEV_URL=https://<dev-ref>.supabase.co
# DEPLOYED_PROJECT_DEV_KEY=your-dev-publishable-or-anon-key
# DEPLOYED_PROJECT_PROD_URL=https://<prod-ref>.supabase.co
# DEPLOYED_PROJECT_PROD_KEY=your-prod-publishable-or-anon-key
